<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madrid Protocol of 1885</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Protocol of Madrid (1885)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> Protocol of 1885 was an agreement signed on 7 March 1885 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Britain and Germany recognised Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the Sulu archipelago, and Spain gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> had been chartered. It settled the rivalry over the region, though the Sulu claim to Sabah lived on.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrid-protocol-of-1885.jpg" alt="Madrid Protocol of 1885" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jjarivera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1848-1899_sulu_ph.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
